#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199393} :activity/id #uuid "f3742955-3684-5655-938c-85f92fc58b37" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048933 :topic/id #uuid "cd37bb30-463c-55ad-8ec5-08aa1d921272" :topic/math-academy-id 4238} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199392 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198442 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198088 :question/id #uuid "ed6483ae-971a-543b-adb6-72ac5809b602" :question/math-academy-id "e-12662"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9d4e49c-202b-564f-839d-64b5d71a3783" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050820}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198560} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198562} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388524} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442114}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Where the Extrema of a Quadratic Form Are Attained on the Unit Sphere"} :step/id #uuid "81974eea-7a73-591a-8f79-590de81df808" :step/math-academy-id 45752 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199396}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199393 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199515 :tutorial/content "If $A$ is the matrix corresponding to the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ then:  \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue $\\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}$ of $A,$ and the maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue $\\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}$ of $A,$ and the minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{min}}.$\n\nWe'll prove this result at the end of the lesson.\n\nSuppose we're given that the maximum value of the quadratic form \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2-10\\: \\! x_1x_2+x_2^2\n$$\n\n \n\nwhen $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1$ is $6.$ Let's use this to find a unit vector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}}$ where this maximum value is attained. \n\nSo first, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNext, we find the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}=6{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ \n\nFinally, dividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v} \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's check that this is indeed the vector we were looking for: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({u}_{\\text{max}}) & ={(\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{2}})}^{2}-10(\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{2}})(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})+{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{10}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =6\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c50ae6a7-1f29-5274-bd82-d2d8acfef6b7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7954 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "08dd4ae6-a6ab-54da-a4ca-840835781b76" :step/math-academy-id 45755 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199394}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199394 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198443 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198089 :question/id #uuid "8a1adb2a-e9c5-52e1-bfa0-6977884fae42" :question/math-academy-id "e-12663"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6115493b-482c-5360-8d19-52582228e21d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050447}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284630} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410198}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Where the Extrema of a Quadratic Form Is Attained on the Unit Circle"} :step/id #uuid "47cdbdea-03d3-506b-a42b-a5ac82733039" :step/math-academy-id 45756 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199392}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199395 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198444 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198090 :question/id #uuid "a6abb2f6-6e59-59ac-8549-62d25b3b6e9b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12664"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "17fd5c3d-d4c6-5f78-b262-fbc6b7209501" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049456}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198685} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284641} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284646} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378962} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410197}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Where the Extrema of a Quadratic Form Is Attained Over an Ellipse"} :step/id #uuid "d2f76172-74c1-501b-a19c-68e6454733cb" :step/math-academy-id 45757 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199404}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199396 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199516 :tutorial/content "Suppose we're given that the maximum value of the quadratic form \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2-2x_1x_2+x_2^2,\n$$\n\n over the ellipse $4x_1^2+x_2^2=4$ equals $5.$ How can we find a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ at which this maximum is attained?\n\nWe can find this vector by applying a suitable change of variable. This change of variable should transform our problem into one where we need to maximize a related quadratic form $Q'$ on the unit circle. Let's see how this is done.\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n{x_1^2}+ \\dfrac{x_2^2}{2^2}=1\n$$\n\nwhich  represents an ellipse with semiaxes $a=1$ (along the $x_1$-axis) and $b=2$ (along the $x_2$-axis). \n\nIf we introduce new variables\n\n$$\ny_1={x_1}, \\qquad y_2 = \\dfrac{x_2}{2},\n$$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into $y_1^2+y_2^2 = 1,$ or $\\|\\mathbf{y}\\|=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $\\mathbf{x} = P \\mathbf{y}.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x} = (P\\mathbf{y})^T A \\, (P\\mathbf{y}) = \\mathbf{y}^T \\! (P^TAP) \\mathbf{y}=Q'(\\mathbf{y}).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since $P^T=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in the new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, the goal is to maximize $Q'(\\mathbf{y})$ when $\\| \\mathbf{y} \\|=1,$ which we do using our usual methods.  \n\nSince we are told that the maximum value of the quadratic form is $5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\max \\{ Q'(\\mathbf{y}) \\: | \\: \\| \\mathbf{y} \\|=1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\max} = 5.\n$$\n\nNext, we find the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}=5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-5I)\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v} \\| = \\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{max}} & =P{u}_{\\text{max}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e750f422-509a-598a-858a-c11360c80eea" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7955 :tutorial/title "Constrained Optimization of Quadratic Forms Over Ellipses"} :step/id #uuid "f3b4db0d-8fec-573f-8597-7e9b0d0a4474" :step/math-academy-id 45758 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199395}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199404 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199547 :tutorial/content "To complete the picture, let's prove the main theorem of this lesson:\n\n> *The maximum value of a quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=\\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x},$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A{:}$* \n> \n> $$\n> \\max \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}\n> $$\n> \n> \n> *The maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}}.$*\n\n**Note:** We'll show only the first part (about the maximum value) since a similar argument can be used to prove the second part (about the minimum value).\n\nLet $A$ be a real $n \\times n$ symmetric matrix corresponding to a quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x)\\mathbin.$ As a result, there must be an *orthonormal* matrix $P$ such that \n\n- $D=P^{-1}AP=P^TAP,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\n- The diagonal entries of $D$ are the eigenvalues of $A$. Without loss of generality, we can assume that the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1 \\geq \\lambda_2 \\geq \\ldots \\geq \\lambda_n,$ where $\\lambda_{\\textrm{max}} = \\lambda_1.$\n\n- The columns $\\mathbf{u}_1,\\mathbf{u}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{u}_n$ of $P$ form an orthonormal set of unit eigenvectors of $A.$ So, $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}} = \\mathbf{u}_1.$\n\nTake $\\mathbf x=P\\mathbf y.$ Since, $P$ is orthonormal, we have\n\n$$\n\\|\\mathbf x\\| = \\|P \\mathbf y \\| = \\|\\mathbf y\\|\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf x) = \\mathbf x^T\\!A\\mathbf x = (P\\mathbf y)^T\\!A(P\\mathbf y) = \\mathbf{y}^T (P^T\\!AP) \\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{y}^T D \\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nfor all $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude the following:\n\n- $\\|\\mathbf x\\| =1 \\Longleftrightarrow \\|\\mathbf y\\| =1$ for all $\\mathbf y$ such that $\\mathbf x=P\\mathbf y$\n\n- Restricted to the unit sphere of $\\Bbb R^n,$ the images of the quadratic forms $Q(\\mathbf x)$  and $Q'(\\mathbf y)= \\mathbf y^T D\\mathbf y$ coincide: \n\n$$\n\\{\\mathbf x^TA\\mathbf x: \\: \\: \\|\\mathbf x\\|=1\\}= \\{\\mathbf y^TD\\mathbf y: \\: \\: \\|\\mathbf y\\|=1\\}  \\subset \\mathbb R\n$$\n\nNow, for any unit vector $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={Q}^{'}(y) \\\\  & ={\\lambda }_{1}{y}_{1}^{2}+{\\lambda }_{2}{y}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{\\lambda }_{n}{y}_{n}^{2} \\\\  & \\le {\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\\,{y}_{1}^{2}+{\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\\,{y}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\\,{y}_{n}^{2} \\\\  & ={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}({y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{y}_{n}^{2}) \\\\  & ={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\\,‖y{‖}^{2} \\\\  & ={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have that  that \n\n$$\nM = \\max \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\max \\{ Q'(\\mathbf{y}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{y} \\| = 1 \\} \\le \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nOn the other hand, if we take $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & … & 0\\end{aligned}]$ then $Q(P\\mathbf{e}_1) = Q'(\\mathbf{e}_1) = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}.$ So, we conclude that $M = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}.$\n\nFinally, since $\\mathbf{u}_1 = P\\mathbf{e}_1$ we have that \n$Q(\\mathbf u_1) =  \\mathbf e_1^T D \\mathbf e_1 = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}.$ \nTherefore, the maximum value is attained at $\\mathbf u_{\\textrm{max}}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "364005f5-f0fc-5d17-9d6b-288d52abe715" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8176 :tutorial/title "Proof of the Main Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "3a8e9986-4b85-5793-91b8-d47f73b67205" :step/math-academy-id 54038}] :activity/title "Constrained Optimization of Quadratic Forms: Determining Where Extrema are Attained" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198510}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410202} :activity/id #uuid "f2727b60-7a6b-5f3a-a237-6a67bfb8139d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048933 :topic/id #uuid "cd37bb30-463c-55ad-8ec5-08aa1d921272" :topic/math-academy-id 4238} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410199 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410196 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1700f9a6-fd4f-55e6-a5b2-c7c162c8b09c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410167} {:answer/id #uuid "56db98f5-8954-5a74-9db5-ea9ad36d4145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410174} {:answer/id #uuid "5fd6a7be-2123-5e1c-93b5-fa6a69ebca48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410178} {:answer/id #uuid "6f32c544-2ebd-5102-86f5-09f04cfa659e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410179} {:answer/id #uuid "8b0dcfdc-b7d8-5406-814d-16ab7d147b59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410181}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410179} :answer-field/id #uuid "62b7e0f0-3a2a-5bad-a1a2-7313ccefaabb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5e6637da-fa52-5317-a60a-ab7680b44bc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-138128" :question/problem "The minimum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)=7{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{2}^{2}+5{x}_{3}^{2}-4{x}_{2}{x}_{3},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $4.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the minimum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0d7e6a3a-4e90-51f7-9e6d-1f36e15dea08" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410203}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410200 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410197 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27fa6c83-fea5-559c-b3af-e6a7e1618346" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410169} {:answer/id #uuid "3bb153c6-9455-5e09-b9d4-e4c936682c6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410172} {:answer/id #uuid "566bcdea-96b1-557a-bdcd-81a53378a8b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410173} {:answer/id #uuid "a93b100e-c392-5adc-a3ed-bee963bcaac6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410185} {:answer/id #uuid "b119d151-632a-5e08-8a6e-94f8c7ba763c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410186}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410169} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5b0d1a4-b3d8-5bd2-80e4-83dea5aa94c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "dea55e7b-1cc0-500b-8640-ae39dddce75c" :question/math-academy-id "q-170560" :question/problem "The maximum value of $Q(x)=4{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2},$ when $9{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}=36,$ is $25.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=3$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}=\\frac{{x}_{2}}{3},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual.\n\nSince we are told that the maximum value of the quadratic form is $25,$ we have\n\n$$\nmax{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1}={\\lambda }_{max}=25.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=25:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-25I & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{aligned}]-25[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -16\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-25I)y=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=5,$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{max}} & =P{u}_{\\text{max}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3781c4aa-7e7b-5b51-9af4-d354711b0091"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410201 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410195 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1579c753-1597-556d-8bd8-51a8deed3084" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410166} {:answer/id #uuid "1c96736a-b709-578c-8d05-cc73305aecdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410168} {:answer/id #uuid "31146e93-a5b1-5fe1-90a1-7f9c284f1056" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410171} {:answer/id #uuid "5dd5c912-e67d-530f-a2c1-baaeaac814c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410177} {:answer/id #uuid "a21806c6-2164-532f-ac84-142d42881117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410184}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410168} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dc24884-5d5e-5b11-b5e7-3763c0022ada" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410191}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3f433543-b9f1-5f20-b0ed-749a73af3fa7" :question/math-academy-id "q-170506" :question/problem "The maximum value of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+16{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2},$ when ${x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}=4,$ is $20.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=1$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}={x}_{2},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 16 \\\\ 8 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 16 \\\\ 16 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual.\n\nSince we are told that the maximum value of the quadratic form is $20,$ we have\n\n$$\nmax\\{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{max}=20.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=20:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-20I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 16 \\\\ 16 & 4\\end{aligned}]-20[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 16 \\\\ 16 & -16\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-20I)y=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get  \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{max}} & =P{u}_{\\text{max}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "80ab5b9e-fd44-5cd4-b555-7638c14ed974" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410200}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410202 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198560 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5991713d-c756-5033-8e5a-4d4d4ed69367" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410175} {:answer/id #uuid "5dd4da5f-a084-569f-9c7d-65ba6f2fa2d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410176} {:answer/id #uuid "7a09264a-e062-5b32-b960-ce1fdd0596f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410180} {:answer/id #uuid "a18e2980-f7b3-5399-bdf5-0e6bab2f2d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410183} {:answer/id #uuid "f781d714-082f-5b5c-9033-4f6f86bf4d82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410188}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410176} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a69a67c-d671-56dc-a201-31f05adf2afb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "13f589cb-239b-530f-99ce-e58d907d8d98" :question/math-academy-id "q-136828" :question/problem "The minimum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)=-{x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $-2.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the minimum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cd806b8f-e6cf-5b59-a4ed-ccc0cfee81a2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410199}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410203 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410198 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0403e816-9a8a-5caa-a60f-9e0db37c2581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410165} {:answer/id #uuid "2ad356f6-e2a2-578b-805e-5d220905af93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410170} {:answer/id #uuid "9cbc00fe-d9dc-5e75-8939-93c74926f323" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410182} {:answer/id #uuid "c87e6493-0b51-5dfe-8402-a4a84620be0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410187} {:answer/id #uuid "fa1e17c0-a680-5fd1-840a-1550ea0d3fa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410189}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410170} :answer-field/id #uuid "59381183-ff09-50a9-9c38-82023491a255" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410190}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f0bc9465-a035-5136-b09d-b009ddf817a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-170400" :question/problem "The minimum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-10{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $-4.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the minimum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe  maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]-(-4)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-4)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$  \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f08afe40-e4fb-52cb-a8c1-acffe9c0cb58" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410201}}] :activity/title "Constrained Optimization of Quadratic Forms: Determining Where Extrema are Attained" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410164}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414677} :activity/id #uuid "dc255669-05a8-5b16-b22c-2690d4b2f343" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048933 :topic/id #uuid "cd37bb30-463c-55ad-8ec5-08aa1d921272" :topic/math-academy-id 4238} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414674 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284641 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01f3ea30-3f9d-5508-ab40-ee2dba4f0888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281708} {:answer/id #uuid "2a5452df-294d-5701-b4af-bbf16d6e0530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282056} {:answer/id #uuid "4e6d984c-3905-5e24-bf07-c5dd11a46a31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282364} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2908d2-0e8d-5999-a64a-a03cd4b68ae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283306} {:answer/id #uuid "c1eb5798-946a-56c8-9937-6c6a91e2207e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283374}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283306} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3ab9ad5-298c-5e24-9d44-dc14f057d818" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "80991c77-0c00-52ac-bae2-41a3b83ff730" :question/math-academy-id "q-170515" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-18{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+81{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that ${x}_{1}^{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2}=9.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 81\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=3$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=1$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{3}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}={x}_{2},$ \n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinates matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 81\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -27 \\\\ -9 & 81\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -27 \\\\ -27 & 81\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual. Calculating the eigenvalues $B,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|B-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}9-\\lambda  & -27 \\\\ -27 & 81-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (9-\\lambda )(81-\\lambda )-729 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-90\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -90) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =0,\\,90\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmax{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=90.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "14546d02-e8ca-5bbd-82f2-37c20dd8c152" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414675}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414675 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410197 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27fa6c83-fea5-559c-b3af-e6a7e1618346" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410169} {:answer/id #uuid "3bb153c6-9455-5e09-b9d4-e4c936682c6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410172} {:answer/id #uuid "566bcdea-96b1-557a-bdcd-81a53378a8b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410173} {:answer/id #uuid "a93b100e-c392-5adc-a3ed-bee963bcaac6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410185} {:answer/id #uuid "b119d151-632a-5e08-8a6e-94f8c7ba763c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410186}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410169} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5b0d1a4-b3d8-5bd2-80e4-83dea5aa94c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "dea55e7b-1cc0-500b-8640-ae39dddce75c" :question/math-academy-id "q-170560" :question/problem "The maximum value of $Q(x)=4{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2},$ when $9{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}=36,$ is $25.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=3$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}=\\frac{{x}_{2}}{3},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual.\n\nSince we are told that the maximum value of the quadratic form is $25,$ we have\n\n$$\nmax{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1}={\\lambda }_{max}=25.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=25:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-25I & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{aligned}]-25[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -16\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-25I)y=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=5,$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{max}} & =P{u}_{\\text{max}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "310e7c10-e8a2-5719-93d8-e4b398b84456"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414676 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389629 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33fcc9d7-da26-566b-892f-8ca0dccbf1